========================================================================================================================
                              CREATING A NEW NETWORK XML FILE & ATTACHING IT TO A VM
========================================================================================================================

PHASE 1: CREATING AND ACTIVATING THE NEW ISOLATED NETWORK XML
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1   | Create a new file named /tmp/custom-net.xml using a text editor, and copy the following network template:
         |
         | <network>
         |   <name>custom-isolated-net</name>
         |   <bridge name='virbr9' stp='on' delay='0'/>
         |   <domain name='custom.local'/>
         |   <ip address='192.168.150.1' netmask='255.255.255.0'>
         |     <dhcp>
         |       <range start='192.168.150.10' end='192.168.150.100'/>
         |     </dhcp>
         |   </ip>
         | </network>
         |
Step 2   | Register and permanently define the network configuration:
         | virsh net-define /tmp/custom-net.xml
         |
Step 3   | Start the network to create the live Linux network bridge interface:
         | virsh net-start custom-isolated-net
         |
Step 4   | Set the network to start automatically when the host server boots up:
         | virsh net-autostart custom-isolated-net
         |
Step 5   | Verify the network state is "active" and autostart is marked "yes":
         | virsh net-list --all

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 2: ATTACHING THE NEW NETWORK INTERFACE TO A SPECIFIC VM
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Use this method to inject the new network layout live into an active VM and commit it to the configuration registry.

Command  | virsh attach-interface --domain my-vm \
         |                        --type network \
         |                        --source custom-isolated-net \
         |                        --model virtio \
         |                        --live \
         |                        --config
         |
Fields   | --domain  : The exact name of your targeted virtual machine (e.g., my-vm).
         | --type    : Specifying "network" maps it directly to a virsh-defined pool.
         | --source  : The exact network name declared in the XML (`custom-isolated-net`).
         | --model   : "virtio" is used for high-performance paravirtualized network drivers.
         | --live    : Hot-plugs the network interface card immediately into the running VM.
         | --config  : Saves the hardware entry permanently to the VM's operational XML manifest.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PHASE 3: POST-ATTACHMENT VERIFICATION WORKFLOW
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1   | List all network interfaces assigned to the target VM to verify the placement:
         | virsh domiflist my-vm
         | Expected output will show "custom-isolated-net" with a newly assigned MAC address.
         |
Step 2   | (Optional) Retrieve the dynamic IP address the VM claims from the new network pool:
         | virsh net-dhcp-leases custom-isolated-net
